title: A counter you can pause
mode: draw
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
parts: dff and chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
A counter should count only when told to. With `en` (enable) on it counts as before; with `en` off it holds its number.

Use the "flip only when asked" idea for each bit:

- `q0` flips when `en` is on: the new `q0` is `q0` XOR `en`
- `q1` flips when `en` is on **and** `q0` is 1: the new `q1` is `q1` XOR (`en` AND `q0`)
---- task
Make `q1 q0` count up one step per tick while `en` is on, and hold while it is off.
---- start
input en 0 0
clock clk 0 260
led q0 580 40
led q1 580 200
---- reference
input en
clock clk
led q0, q1
wire a[1], b[1]
a = dff(a ^ en, clk)
b = dff(b ^ (en & a), clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
---- answer
input en 0 0
clock clk 0 260
chip x0 my_xor 160 30
dff f0 320 40
and g1 300 150
chip x1 my_xor 400 190
dff f1 480 200
led q0 580 40
led q1 580 200
f0.q -> x0.a
en -> x0.b
x0.y -> f0.d
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> q0
en -> g1.0
f0.q -> g1.1
f1.q -> x1.a
g1 -> x1.b
x1.y -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
---- hint1
Bit 0 is the "flip only when asked" circuit with `en` as its `t`.
---- hint2
Bit 1 is the same, but its "asked" is `en` AND `q0`: an AND gate feeding the second my_xor.
---- solution
Flip-flop 0 takes `q0` XOR `en`. An AND gives `en` AND `q0`, and flip-flop 1 takes `q1` XOR that. With `en` off, neither changes.
---- done
Every timer in a computer has an enable like this, so a program can start and stop it.
